1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,193
Everything so far only listened.

2
00:00:02,193 --> 00:00:05,413
Before anything can be sent, there is one gate.

3
00:00:05,413 --> 00:00:10,140
ARM TX is off by default and nothing leaves the tool while it
is off:

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:14,457
not injection, not replay, not fuzzing, not gateway
forwarding,

5
00:00:14,457 --> 00:00:16,855
and not diagnostic requests either.

6
00:00:16,855 --> 00:00:18,431
The status bar says so.

7
00:00:18,431 --> 00:00:22,816
Turning it on requires confirming a warning that names the
risk,

8
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:27,064
and turning it off again does not merely block the next frame,

9
00:00:27,064 --> 00:00:29,736
it stops anything already transmitting.

10
00:00:29,736 --> 00:00:35,167
With transmit armed, and only on an isolated bench, the
INJECTION tab can send.

11
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:38,605
Set a signal to a physical value and send it once,

12
00:00:38,605 --> 00:00:43,624
or loop it at a chosen period with the counter and checksum
kept correct.

13
00:00:43,624 --> 00:00:48,848
Replay a capture back onto the bus at real speed or faster,
with a scrubber.

14
00:00:48,848 --> 00:00:52,355
Define trigger rules that fire on a byte condition.

15
00:00:52,355 --> 00:00:58,267
Sweep an actuator between limits with a watchdog that aborts
if a message goes silent.

16
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:02,736
And run scripted test sequences of inject, wait and assert
steps.

17
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:07,032
The GATEWAY tab bridges two CAN channels and puts you in
between.

18
00:01:07,032 --> 00:01:08,816
Rules are applied in order:

19
00:01:08,816 --> 00:01:14,566
pass a message through, block it, or modify a byte or the
arbitration ID as it crosses.

20
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:19,721
That is how you test what an ECU does when a message it
depends on disappears,

21
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:21,968
or arrives with a different value.

22
00:01:21,968 --> 00:01:27,982
It needs two hardware channels, and like everything else it
will not forward a single frame

23
00:01:27,982 --> 00:01:29,568
until transmit is armed.

24
00:01:29,568 --> 00:01:33,638
The OBD-II tab is the standardised subset any car will answer.

25
00:01:33,638 --> 00:01:36,593
It discovers which PIDs the vehicle supports,

26
00:01:36,593 --> 00:01:41,517
walking the continuation windows rather than assuming the
first thirty-two,

27
00:01:41,517 --> 00:01:45,653
and then polls the ones you pick and shows them as live
gauges:

28
00:01:45,653 --> 00:01:49,920
engine speed, coolant temperature, road speed, throttle
position.

29
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:53,749
The AI ENGINE is optional and off unless you configure it.

30
00:01:53,749 --> 00:01:57,645
It sends one message ID's statistics to Anthropic, to Groq,

31
00:01:57,645 --> 00:02:03,587
or to a local Ollama model that never leaves your machine, and
asks for an interpretation.

32
00:02:03,587 --> 00:02:08,407
What makes it useful is that the offline analysis goes with
the question:

33
00:02:08,407 --> 00:02:12,236
the byte roles, the detected checksum, the message period.

34
00:02:12,236 --> 00:02:16,131
The model reasons about measured facts rather than raw hex.

35
00:02:16,131 --> 00:02:22,403
Its answers are still suggestions, and accepting one into the
DBC is refused unless it actually

36
00:02:22,403 --> 00:02:23,856
states a bit position.

37
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:26,765
Connected to a real bus, frames stream in live.

38
00:02:26,765 --> 00:02:31,779
One receive thread reads the adapter and hands frames to
everyone who needs them,

39
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:36,608
so a diagnostic scan and the live table never steal responses
from each other.

40
00:02:36,608 --> 00:02:40,384
Frames go into a capped buffer that appends in constant time,

41
00:02:40,384 --> 00:02:44,036
so a busy bus does not slow the tool down as the log grows:

42
00:02:44,036 --> 00:02:48,864
the frame counter and the rate keep climbing while the table
stays responsive.

43
00:02:48,864 --> 00:02:49,962
That is the loop:

44
00:02:49,962 --> 00:02:54,870
load a capture, let the offline analysis narrow it down,
define the signals,

45
00:02:54,870 --> 00:02:59,456
verify them by decoding real frames, and export to whatever
comes next.

46
00:02:59,456 --> 00:03:04,623
A project file saves the frames, the signal definitions and
your notes together.

47
00:03:04,623 --> 00:03:09,725
A REST API and an MCP server expose the same analysis to
scripts and to agents.

48
00:03:09,725 --> 00:03:13,536
And the one rule that does not change is the transmit gate:

49
00:03:13,536 --> 00:03:19,542
it is off until you turn it on, and you should only turn it on
when the bus you are connected

50
00:03:19,542 --> 00:03:20,640
to is on a bench.
